A style field written with its unit, on android.

`8px` is a unit value, and Kotlin spells one as the `Measurement` data class
this target emits. Every place a Style field lands on Compose appends `.dp` or
`.sp` to it, so the field came out as `Measurement(px = 8.0).dp` -- an
extension nothing declares, and a file that does not compile. The same numbers
written bare worked, which is why nothing had caught it: the harness compiles
what the fixtures write, and the fixtures wrote `padding=8`.

Both spellings are below and both are the same number, which is the claim: a
measurement reaching a Compose argument is its magnitude in px, as it is
everywhere else this platform answers one.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `Measurement\(px` -- a measurement reaching a Compose argument is reduced to its magnitude, not spelled as the data class
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

window #home(title="units") {
    vbox(style={padding=8px, gap=4px}) {
        text(value="with units", style={fontSize=32px})
        text(value="bare", style={fontSize=32})
        button(text="rounded", style={borderRadius=12px, width=100px, height=40px})
    }
}
